A knob for the CPU thread count, so it can be measured instead of assumed.

qwentts.cpp fixes the count at hardware_concurrency() / 2 and exposes no
parameter. The reasoning is an SMT desktop: GEMM shares SIMD units between
hyperthreads, so one thread per physical core is best.

The target device is a big.LITTLE phone. There is no second thread per core to
halve away, and the cores left in are not equal. The runtime reports twelve
logical CPUs there, so the heuristic picks six; the llama lane on the same
device measured both prefill and decode flat from four threads up, which is
what running out of fast cores looks like.

That argument turned out to predict a difference that is not there. Swept on the
phone, four, six and eight land within about 2% of each other, inside the drift
five consecutive runs accumulate from heat, and only two threads is clearly
worse. Upstream's choice costs nothing here. The patch stays because the next
device is not this one, and because the alternative is trusting the argument
again.

QWEN_N_THREADS, read at qt_init only, because that is where the count is
consumed -- backend_init calls this once and hands the value to
ggml_backend_set_n_threads on a backend that then lives for the handle. A sweep
therefore reloads, unlike the llama one. Unset or unparseable leaves upstream's
heuristic exactly as it was.

--- a/src/backend.h
+++ b/src/backend.h
@@ -26,7 +26,17 @@ struct BackendPair {
 // Physical core count heuristic (logical / 2 for HT/SMT).
 // Used for GGML CPU thread count: GEMM shares SIMD units across hyperthreads,
 // so one thread per physical core is optimal.
+//
+// QWEN_N_THREADS overrides it. The heuristic assumes SMT, which a phone does
+// not have; see third/patches/ in the yoai repo for the measurement that
+// asked for this.
 static int backend_cpu_n_threads(void) {
+    if (const char * env = std::getenv("QWEN_N_THREADS")) {
+        const int forced = std::atoi(env);
+        if (forced > 0) {
+            return forced;
+        }
+    }
     int n = (int) std::thread::hardware_concurrency() / 2;
     return n > 0 ? n : 1;
 }
